hamilton-jacobi-theory-identity-1-ce2569.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit where the Hamiltonian is time-independent (
\frac{\partial H}{\partial t} = 0
), the action S can be separated into a time-dependent part and a time-independent part: S(q, t) = W(q) - Et. Substituting this into the Hamilton-Jacobi equation leads to \(E = H\left(q, \frac{\partial W}{\partial q}\right)\), where E is the conserved energy and W(q) is the characteristic function. In the classical limit (e.g., as Planck's constant approaches zero in its quantum mechanical analogue), the Hamilton-Jacobi equation reduces to the classical equations of motion.

